   crossing convention: gravitational Compton phi(p) g(k, +2) -> phi(p') g(k', lf), m = 1, omega = 0.7, lab:
      lf = +2: |M|/(8 pi G) = 1.368596657
      lf = -2: |M|/(8 pi G) = 0.328989928
      S10 crossed (s,t,u)_gg = (t_C, u_C, s_C): same-helicity form = 0.328989928, opposite-helicity form = 1.368596657
      => Compton helicity FLIP (+2 -> -2) = the S10 m^4 ('A++') form; crossing the outgoing graviton to an
      incoming one reverses its helicity, so the flip becomes gg -> phi phi with SAME physical helicities.
      Hence B&O's A^t_{++} (m^4) is labelled by the physical helicities of the two INCOMING gravitons, not by
      the (in, out) labels of the Compton amplitude it was crossed from - unlike eq. 6, whose nonzero labels
      only make sense as spin projections on a common axis (taskA1).

